.SH NAME
md_frontend_2node__modules_2json-stable-stringify-without-jsonify_2readme \- json-stable-stringify 
.PP


.PP
This is the same as https://github.com/substack/json-stable-stringify but it doesn't depend on libraries without licenses (jsonify)\&.

.PP
deterministic version of \fRJSON\&.stringify()\fP so you can get a consistent hash from stringified results

.PP
You can also pass in a custom comparison function\&.

.PP
\fR\fP
.PP
\fR\fP.SH "example"
.PP
.PP
.nf
 js
var stringify = require('json\-stable\-stringify');
var obj = { c: 8, b: [{z:6,y:5,x:4},7], a: 3 };
console\&.log(stringify(obj));
.fi
.PP

.PP
output:

.PP
.PP
.nf
{"a":3,"b":[{"x":4,"y":5,"z":6},7],"c":8}
.fi
.PP
.SH "methods"
.PP
.PP
.nf
 js
var stringify = require('json\-stable\-stringify')
.fi
.PP
.SS "var str = stringify(obj, opts)"
Return a deterministic stringified string \fRstr\fP from the object \fRobj\fP\&.
.SS "options"
.SS "cmp"
If \fRopts\fP is given, you can supply an \fRopts\&.cmp\fP to have a custom comparison function for object keys\&. Your function \fRopts\&.cmp\fP is called with these parameters:

.PP
.PP
.nf
 js
opts\&.cmp({ key: akey, value: avalue }, { key: bkey, value: bvalue })
.fi
.PP

.PP
For example, to sort on the object key names in reverse order you could write:

.PP
.PP
.nf
 js
var stringify = require('json\-stable\-stringify');

var obj = { c: 8, b: [{z:6,y:5,x:4},7], a: 3 };
var s = stringify(obj, function (a, b) {
    return a\&.key < b\&.key ? 1 : \-1;
});
console\&.log(s);
.fi
.PP

.PP
which results in the output string:

.PP
.PP
.nf
{"c":8,"b":[{"z":6,"y":5,"x":4},7],"a":3}
.fi
.PP

.PP
Or if you wanted to sort on the object values in reverse order, you could write:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var stringify = require('json\-stable\-stringify');

var obj = { d: 6, c: 5, b: [{z:3,y:2,x:1},9], a: 10 };
var s = stringify(obj, function (a, b) {
    return a\&.value < b\&.value ? 1 : \-1;
});
console\&.log(s);
.fi
.PP

.PP
which outputs:

.PP
.PP
.nf
{"d":6,"c":5,"b":[{"z":3,"y":2,"x":1},9],"a":10}
.fi
.PP
.SS "space"
If you specify \fRopts\&.space\fP, it will indent the output for pretty-printing\&. Valid values are strings (e\&.g\&. \fR{space: \\t}\fP) or a number of spaces (\fR{space: 3}\fP)\&.

.PP
For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var obj = { b: 1, a: { foo: 'bar', and: [1, 2, 3] } };
var s = stringify(obj, { space: '  ' });
console\&.log(s);
.fi
.PP

.PP
which outputs:

.PP
.PP
.nf
{
  "a": {
    "and": [
      1,
      2,
      3
    ],
    "foo": "bar"
  },
  "b": 1
}
.fi
.PP
.SS "replacer"
The replacer parameter is a function \fRopts\&.replacer(key, value)\fP that behaves the same as the replacer \fRfrom the core JSON object\fP\&.
.SH "install"
.PP
With \fRnpm\fP do:

.PP
.PP
.nf
npm install json\-stable\-stringify
.fi
.PP
